  "text_input": "Mrs. Uma, a 40-year-old female, presented with acute onset loose stools (5-6 episodes) and nausea for one day. The stools were watery, non-bloody, and non-mucoid, associated with colicky lower abdominal pain. She was admitted to the MICU in hypovolemic shock and managed with vasopressor support (Noradrenaline), intravenous fluids, and antibiotics (Ciplox, Metronidazole). Investigations revealed a total bilirubin of 0.3 mg/dL, albumin of 3.2 g/dL, and GRBS of 106 mg/dL. USG abdomen showed Grade 1 fatty liver, and 2D Echo showed an EF of 58%. The patient's symptoms resolved, and she was discharged on oral medications including Ciplox-TZ and Sporolac-DS.",
